{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "5: Add Human-in-the-Loop Authorization",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "What you will build",
      "description": "## What you will build\n\nIn **Add the Standard Tools (Tour 3)** you saw that Z-Assistant processes refunds immediately — no human oversight.\n\nIn this tour you will fix that by wiring up CIBA authorization wrappers around the two sensitive tools:\n\n- `processRefundTool` — financial refunds\n- `getCustomerProfileTool` — private customer PII\n\nWhen a CIBA-wrapped tool is called, the `@auth0/ai-vercel` SDK:\n\n1. Keeps the SSE connection open (polling mode).\n2. Calls Auth0's backchannel authentication endpoint.\n3. Auth0 pushes a notification to the logged-in user's Guardian app.\n4. If the user **approves**, the tool executes and the response appears automatically.\n5. If the user **denies**, an `AccessDeniedInterrupt` is thrown and the agent stops.\n\nYou will make seven small code changes across two files."
    },
    {
      "file": "z-assistant/src/lib/auth0-ai.ts",
      "description": "## Import Auth0AI and getUserID\n\nThe `auth0-ai.ts` module is where we configure the CIBA wrappers. Right now it only contains commented-out TODOs.\n\nUncomment the import statement by replacing the TODO block with:\n\n```ts\nimport { Auth0AI } from '@auth0/ai-vercel';\nimport { getUserID } from './auth0.js';\n```\n\n`Auth0AI` is the main entry point from the `@auth0/ai-vercel` SDK. `getUserID` reads the Auth0 `sub` (user ID) from the OIDC session — CIBA uses this to identify who should receive the push notification.",
      "line": 18,
      "title": "Import Auth0AI and getUserID",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 18,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 21,
          "character": 100
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "z-assistant/src/lib/auth0-ai.ts",
      "description": "## Initialize the Auth0AI client\n\nUncomment the constructor by replacing the TODO block with:\n\n```ts\nconst auth0AI = new Auth0AI();\n```\n\n`Auth0AI` reads your Auth0 credentials (`AUTH0_DOMAIN`, `AUTH0_CLIENT_ID`, `AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET`) from environment variables automatically. This single instance is shared across all wrappers in this file.",
      "line": 21,
      "title": "Initialize the Auth0AI client",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 21,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 23,
          "character": 100
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "z-assistant/src/lib/auth0-ai.ts",
      "description": "## Create withRefundApproval\n\nUncomment the `withRefundApproval` export by replacing the TODO block with:\n\n```ts\nexport const withRefundApproval = auth0AI.withAsyncAuthorization({\n  userID: () => getUserID(),\n  bindingMessage: (toolArgs: { orderId: string; amount: number; reason: string }) =>\n    `Approve refund of ${toolArgs.amount.toFixed(2)} USD for order ${toolArgs.orderId}`,\n  scopes: ['openid', 'profile'],\n  onAuthorizationRequest: async (_authReq: any, creds: Promise<any>) => {\n    sseStore.getStore()?.('ciba_pending', { message: 'An approval request has been sent to your device. Approve it to process the refund.' });\n    await creds;\n  },\n  onUnauthorized: (err: unknown) => { throw err; },\n});\n```\n\n**Key parameters:**\n\n- `userID` — called at runtime to get the logged-in user's Auth0 `sub`. Auth0 uses this to route the Guardian push to the right device.\n- `bindingMessage` — the text shown on the Guardian notification, including the exact amount and order number.\n- `scopes` — the OAuth scopes requested for the CIBA authorization grant.\n- `onAuthorizationRequest` — switches the wrapper to **polling mode**. Instead of throwing an interrupt and closing the connection, the library loops in the background until Guardian approval arrives. The callback fires immediately so we can notify the browser via `sseStore`, then `await creds` blocks until the user approves. The SSE connection stays open the whole time.\n- `onUnauthorized` — called for non-CIBA errors; rethrowing surfaces them as `tool-error` stream events.",
      "line": 31,
      "title": "Create withRefundApproval",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 31,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 41,
          "character": 100
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "z-assistant/src/lib/auth0-ai.ts",
      "description": "## Create withCustomerDataApproval\n\nUncomment the `withCustomerDataApproval` export by replacing the TODO block with:\n\n```ts\nexport const withCustomerDataApproval = auth0AI.withAsyncAuthorization({\n  userID: () => getUserID(),\n  bindingMessage: (toolArgs: { customerId: string }) =>\n    `Allow Z-Assistant to access private profile data for customer ${toolArgs.customerId}`,\n  scopes: ['openid', 'profile'],\n  onAuthorizationRequest: async (_authReq: any, creds: Promise<any>) => {\n    sseStore.getStore()?.('ciba_pending', { message: 'An approval request has been sent to your device. Approve it to access the customer profile.' });\n    await creds;\n  },\n  onUnauthorized: (err: unknown) => { throw err; },\n});\n```\n\nThis wrapper protects the customer PII endpoint. The binding message tells the approver exactly which customer's private data is being requested, so they can make an informed decision.\n\nYour `auth0-ai.ts` is now complete. Next we'll attach these wrappers to the tool definitions.",
      "line": 58,
      "title": "Create withCustomerDataApproval",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 58,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 68,
          "character": 100
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "z-assistant/src/tools/retailzero.ts",
      "description": "## Import the CIBA wrappers\n\nNow open `retailzero.ts` and uncomment the import for the two wrappers. Replace the TODO comment on line 3 with:\n\n```ts\nimport { withRefundApproval, withCustomerDataApproval } from '../lib/auth0-ai.js';\n```\n\nThis makes the CIBA wrappers available to wrap the sensitive tool definitions below.",
      "line": 3,
      "title": "Import the CIBA wrappers",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 3,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 4,
          "character": 100
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "z-assistant/src/tools/retailzero.ts",
      "description": "## Wrap processRefundTool\n\nSince issuing refunds is a sensitive operation, we need human (e.g., a manager) approval before the agent issues the refund. To do this, we will wrap the `tool` object associated with the `processRefundTool` in our new `withRefundApproval` function. This function will kick off the CIBA flow and require human-in-the-loop authorization before the tool's `execute` function can run. This prevents the agent from issuing the refund without approval.\n\n### 1. Select the **Insert code** link below to apply this change to `processRefundTool`:\n\n```ts\nexport const processRefundTool = withRefundApproval(tool({\n    description:\n    'Process a refund for a delivered order.',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    orderId: z.string().describe('The order ID to refund (e.g. ORD-1001)'),\n    amount: z.number().positive().describe('Refund amount in USD'),\n    reason: z.string().describe('Reason for the refund'),\n  }),\n  execute: async ({ orderId, amount, reason }) => {\n    const res = await fetch(`${RETAILZERO_API}/api/refunds`, {\n      method: 'POST',\n      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ orderId, amount, reason }),\n    });\n    if (!res.ok) {\n      const body = await res.json().catch(() => ({}));\n      return { error: (body as any).error ?? 'Refund request failed.' };\n    }\n    return await res.json();\n  },\n}));\n```",
      "line": 110,
      "title": "Wrap processRefundTool",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 89,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 110,
          "character": 4
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "z-assistant/src/tools/retailzero.ts",
      "description": "## Wrap getCustomerProfileTool\n\nWe also don't want the agent to expose customer PII without approval. Similar to the last step, we'll wrap the `tool` object associated with `getCustomerProfileTool` with the `withCustomerDataApproval` function to kick off the CIBA flow before the tool's `execute` function can be triggered.\n\n### 1. Select the **Insert code** link below to apply this change to `getCustomerProfileTool`:\n\n```ts\nexport const getCustomerProfileTool = withCustomerDataApproval(tool({\n  description:\n    'Retrieve a customer\\'s full profile including private contact details (phone, address) and payment information.',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    customerId: z.string().describe('The customer ID (e.g. CUST-001)'),\n  }),\n  execute: async ({ customerId }) => {\n    const res = await fetch(`${RETAILZERO_API}/api/customers/${customerId}/profile`);\n    if (!res.ok) return { error: `Customer ${customerId} not found.` };\n    return await res.json();\n  },\n}));\n```",
      "line": 126,
      "title": "Wrap getCustomerProfileTool",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 111,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 127,
          "character": 47
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Test the CIBA flow",
      "description": "## Test the CIBA flow\n\n1. Make sure the **RetailZero API** is still running on port 3001. \n> If it isn't you can [select this link](command:codetour.sendTextToTerminal?[\"cd retailzero-api && npm run dev\"]) to run it again.\n> Ensure the port 3001 for the API is public under the PORTS tab.\n\n2. [Start Z-Assistant](command:workbench.action.debug.start) and open it in the browser by using the globe icon under the PORTS tab for port 3000.\n\n3. Send this prompt:\n   > _\"Process a refund of $216.99 for order ORD-1001, reason: customer not satisfied.\"_\n\n4. The status bar will show **\"Using processRefund…\"** then switch to **\"An approval request has been sent to your device.\"**\n\n5. Check your phone; you should see a **Guardian push notification**:\n   > _\"Approve refund of 216.99 USD for order ORD-1001\"_\n\n6. Select **Approve** in Auth0 Guardian.\n7. Since Z-Assistant periodically polls the CIBA flow, it will automatically resumes and completes the refund once your approval is in the CIBA response.\n\n---\n\n**Optionally, you can test denial:**\n\n1. Ask for another refund and tap **Deny** on Guardian instead.\n2. Z-Assistant should respond that the authorization was denied and the refund was not processed.\n\n---\n\n**Congratulations!** Z-Assistant now enforces human-in-the-loop authorization for all sensitive operations. Financial refunds and customer PII access can no longer be executed autonomously — every request requires explicit approval from the authenticated user on their registered device."
    }
  ]
}